menu "HAL NFC"

    config HAL_NFC_UART_NUM
        int "UART port connected to the PN532"
        range 1 2
        default 1
        help
            UART port that drives the PN532 in HSU mode. UART0 stays free for
            the console.

    config HAL_NFC_TX_GPIO
        int "GPIO for ESP32 TX (PN532 RX)"
        range 0 SOC_GPIO_OUT_RANGE_MAX
        default 17
        help
            GPIO that carries data from the ESP32 to the PN532 RX pin.

    config HAL_NFC_RX_GPIO
        int "GPIO for ESP32 RX (PN532 TX)"
        range 0 SOC_GPIO_IN_RANGE_MAX
        default 18
        help
            GPIO that carries data from the PN532 TX pin to the ESP32.

    config HAL_NFC_SDM_BASE_URL
        string "SDM base URL"
        default "https://example.com/tap"
        help
            URL prefix written into the NTAG424 DNA NDEF file. The terminal
            appends "/<device_id>?picc_data=<32 hex>&cmac=<16 hex>" and lets the
            tag fill the placeholders through Secure Dynamic Messaging. The
            backend that receives the tap must serve this path. Do not end the
            value with a slash. The value may be at most 139 characters so the
            whole NDEF file fits one DESFire WriteData frame; the build fails
            when it is longer.

            Key limitation: the terminal authenticates with, and leaves in
            place, the factory all-zero AES-128 keys (key 0 and the SDM file
            read key). It never calls ChangeKey, because an interrupted key
            change can lock a tag for good. Anyone who knows this firmware can
            therefore decrypt picc_data and compute a valid cmac, so SDM proves
            only that a tap happened, not that a genuine tag produced it. A tag
            whose key 0 was changed cannot be configured by this firmware.

endmenu
